WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:05.350
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:09.720
Genetic algorithms help us solve problems
that are very difficult, if not impossible

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.090
to otherwise write programs for.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:16.829
For instance, in this application, we have
to build a simple car model to traverse this

00:00:16.829 --> 00:00:22.210
terrain. Put some number of wheels on it somewhere,
add a set of triangles as a chassis, and off

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:25.839
you go. This is essentially the DNA of a solution.

00:00:25.839 --> 00:00:30.630
The farther it goes, the better the car is,
and the goal is to design the best car you

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:34.750
possibly can.
First, the algorithm will try some random

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:39.990
solutions, and, as it has no idea about the
concept of a car or gravity, it will create

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:42.650
a lot of bad solutions that don't work at
all.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:49.450
However, after a point, it will create something
that is at least remotely similar to a car,

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:53.980
which will immediately perform so much better
than the other solutions in the population.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:01:00.420
A genetic algorithm then creates a new set
of solutions, however, now, not randomly.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:05.780
It respects a rule that we call: survival
of the fittest. Which means the best existing

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:10.630
solutions are taken and mixed together to
breed new solutions that are also expected

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:16.729
to do well. Like in evolution in nature, mutations
can also happen, which means random changes

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:19.869
are applied to the DNA code of a solution.

00:01:19.869 --> 00:01:26.249
We know from nature that evolution works extraordinarily
well, and the more we run this genetic optimization

00:01:26.249 --> 00:01:30.429
program, the better the solutions get. It's
quite delightful for a programmer to see their

00:01:30.429 --> 00:01:35.959
own children trying vigorously and succeeding
at solving a difficult task. Even more so

00:01:35.959 --> 00:01:40.079
if the programmer wouldn't be able to solve
this problem by himself.

00:01:40.079 --> 00:01:43.869
Let's run a quick example.
We start with a set of solutions - the DNA

00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:49.689
of a solution is a set of zeros and ones,
which can encode some decision about the solution,

00:01:49.689 --> 00:01:55.459
whether we turn left or right in a maze, or
it can also be an integer or any real number.

00:01:55.459 --> 00:02:00.499
We then compute how good these solutions are
according to our taste, in the example with

00:02:00.499 --> 00:02:03.240
cars, how far these designs can get.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:11.400
Then, we take, for instance, the best 3 solutions
and co mbine them together to create a new DNA

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.280
Some of the better solutions may remain
in the population unchanged. Then, probabilistically,

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:25.520
random mutations happen to some of the solutions,
which help us explore the vast search space

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:26.120
better.

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:30.030
Rinse and repeat, and there you have it. Genetic
algorithms.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:36.250
I have also coded up a version of Roger Alsing's
EvoLisa problem where the famous Mona Lisa

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:41.840
painting is to be reproduced by a computer
program with a few tens of triangles. The

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:46.820
goal is to paint a version that is as faithful
to the original as possible. This would be

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:52.340
quite difficult for humans, but apparently
a genetic algorithm can deal with this really

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:57.620
well. The code is available for everyone to
learn, experiment, and play with. It's super

00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:58.680
fun.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:03.070
And if you're interested in the concept of
evolution, maybe read the excellent book,

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:05.660
The Blind Watchmaker by Richard Dawkins.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:09.640
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

